\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The cast-iron skillet hissed as a pad of salted butter hit the black surface, melting into a shimmering, hazelnut-scented pool. Arthur nudged two brown eggs against the rim of the counter, cracking them neatly into the pan. They snapped and spat back at him, whites blooming like white dahlias around sunny, trembling yolks.\
Morning light filtered through the dusty kitchen blinds, slicing across the worn oak table where a mug of dark roast coffee steamed. The aroma was rich and slightly bitter, cut only by the sweet, dark scent of blueberry jam resting in a small glass dish nearby.\
He slid two thick slices of sourdough into the toaster. A minute later, they popped up, pale gold and steaming. He smeared them generously with salted butter that pooled into the airy pockets, followed by a heavy dollop of jam. Beside them on the plate went the eggs\'97edges crisp and laced with brown, centers still soft and dark orange\'97and three strips of thick-cut bacon, glistening and maple-glazed.\
Arthur sat down, the quiet of the house wrapping around him like an old coat. He pierced the first yolk with the edge of his toast. It broke silently, rich and golden, running over the sourdough and onto the plate. The first bite was pure contrast: the shatter of toasted crust, the warm velvet of yolk, the punch of salt from the crisp bacon, and the bright, tart sweet of the berries.\
He chewed slowly, taking a sip of scalding coffee right after. It burned slightly on the back of his throat, grounding him completely in the crisp, quiet start of the day. No news, no notifications, no rushed footsteps. Just the simple, perfect math of cast iron, butter, and time.\
